// Package importsettings reads and writes import settings sidecars: a small YAML file beside an image, a sound, a font or a model, named after it with ".import.yaml" appended (Path), that says how game assets imports it, as Godot's .import files do.
//
// An image's sidecar holds its texture compression format, mipmaps, sRGB and sampling filter, which a texture target encodes it with, and the grid an atlas target slices a sprite sheet into frames along. A sound's sidecar holds its output format, sample rate, channels, loudness, bitrate and loop points, which an audio target converts it with. A font's sidecar holds the size, characters and padding a font target bakes it into a bitmap font with, and a model's the scale and whether to keep animations that a model target packs it into one GLB file with. A setting in the sidecar overrides the target's setting in game.yaml, so a person edits one asset's import by editing the file beside it, and game assets rebuilds the targets that read the asset when its sidecar changes. The single-file commands game tex and game audio read the sidecar beside their input too, under the flags given on the command line.
//
// Any asset's sidecar may also hold a credit section: the asset's author, its license as an SPDX license expression (ValidateLicense), where it was obtained, and a notice the license requires to be shipped with it. Package credits lists these in the credits file game release writes beside the release's packages.
//
// Parse decodes a version 1 sidecar and runs Validate, refusing unknown fields, other versions, a sidecar with no section or with more than one of image, audio, font and model, values out of range, and a credit without an author or with a license that is not an SPDX license expression. Encode validates a File and writes deterministic 2-space YAML in a fixed key order, after a first line that points a YAML language server at SchemaURL, which Parse reads back unchanged.
package importsettings
